--TEST--
`secret` is refused at the contagious, neutral and laundering parameters that each accept the same argument marked `tainted`, so the admission moved one axis and not both — `rule:security/unclassified-parameter-refuses-tainted` and `rule:security/secret-qualifier`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The two qualifiers are independent axes (`rule:security/secret-qualifier`), and `rule:security/unclassified-parameter-refuses-tainted`'s
// admission is written for `tainted` alone: whether a neutral parameter may
// launder a `secret` is still that ADR's question to answer. This file pins
// the bound as it stands, so closing it is a visible edit here rather than a
// silent widening -- the accepted rows above each refusal are what make it a
// statement about the axis and not about the member.
tainted string $t = "a";
secret string $s = "a";

// Contagious: the tainted argument is admitted and the answer carries it.
tainted string $joined = Core\Str::join(["x"], $t);
// Neutral: admitted, and the count carries nothing.
uint $length = Core\Str::length($t);
// Launder: admitted, and the answer is plain by the row's own claim.
string $quoted = Core\Regex::quote($t);
echo $joined, $length, $quoted, "\n";

// The same three positions, one axis over. None of them is a way out of
// `secret`, which output, logs and dumps all refuse (`rule:security/secret-qualifier`).
string $joinedSecret = Core\Str::join(["x"], $s);
uint $lengthSecret = Core\Str::length($s);
string $quotedSecret = Core\Regex::quote($s);
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `secret string`
   --> case.nvs:21:46
%A
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `secret string`
   --> case.nvs:22:39
%A
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `secret string`
   --> case.nvs:23:42
%A
